Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make MetroSim
     - run executable with
            ./MetroSim stationsFile passFile [commands]


Program Purpose: Simulate the process of a train in a metro system. 
Simulation can process passengers being added and taken off the train.


Acknowledgements: Caroline for helping me during the Design Check Off. Austin 
and John for helping me with a linker error. Darya helped me with how to get
my code to stop when the last command is not "m f". 


Files: 

main.cpp:
     Main file simple driver function that run the MetroSim simulator.

MetroSim.cpp:
     Implementation of MetroSim class. Main functionality of running the Metro Simulation, moves trains and deal with passengers going on/off the train.

MetroSim.h:
     Interface of MetroSim class.

stations.txt:
     an example file containing a list of stations.

test_commands.txt:
     sample list of commands that you could give to the simulator

the_metroSim:
     reference implementation for students to see how

stations1.txt stations2.txt
     station lists of different lengths to test code

commands1.txt commands2.txt 
     input commands to test the program. 



Note:  the program should handle all cases.

Data Structures:

I used various different data structures in this project.

First I used a list in order to create my PassenegerQueue class. The 
PassengerQueue classes uses the built in c++ std::list in order to create a 
queue where Passenegers are stored and only the front passenger can be 
accessed, the front passenger maybe removed, and a new passenger maybe added to
the back. I used a list here because the complexity of removing an element from 
the front is O(1) for lists and O(n) for vectors so I opted to use the one with
a smaller complexity. Another situation where to use a queue would be in CS 
office hours since only the front needs to be accessed and removed and all 
new students need to be added to the back. A third situation would be in a 
kitchen of dishes to make since only the front needs to be accessed to know 
the next item to make, only the front needs to be removed when item is made, 
and new items need to be added to the back.

The second data structure I used was c++'s built in ArrayList: std::vector.
I used this data structure in order to store all Stations and to do this I 
used a struct called Stations. So my vector was filled with type Station. The 
Struct held the station id, name, and two passenegerQueues. The first 
PassengerQueue stored all passenger who are going to board the train at this 
station and my second PassenegerQueue stored all passengers on the train who
are going to get off it at that station. I used a vector here because the 
complexity of accessing an element is O(1) for vectors and O(n) for lists
so I opted to use the one with a smaller complexity. A vector would be useful 
for the other situations where an expandable and array ius 


Testing:

Passenger and PassengerQueue

For phase 1 I used unit tests to test my PassenegerQueue and Passenger classes. 
I first confirmed that I would be able to create instances of both the classes. 
Then I confirmed that the enqueue() function in the PassenegerQueue works by 
attempting to add a passeneger to the back of the queue. Then, I confirmed that
the front function works. I did this by enqueueuing a couple passengers to the 
queue and then called front() and made sure that the Passenger had the correct 
id number, to station, and from station. Then I tested print(), by enqueuing a 
passenger and confirming that print() returns that passenger's information in 
the correct format. Finally, I tested dequeue() by enqueueing a couple 
passengers, callind dequeue(), and then calling print to see if the first 
passeneger was printed. In this part, I was confused at first how to check 
print since I had to create an output stream to pass into the print() function.

MetroSim

For phase 2, I used the_MetroSim demo and diff checked it with my own program
to test it. I first created a couple different station files with a different
number of station files. Station1.txt has 2 stations and Station2.txt has three
stations. I also used the provided stations file. For each new station file I 
created I also created a commands file: commands1.txt and command2.txt which 
would test stations1.txt and stations2.txt respectively. Within these files I
tested if my demo was able to wrap around to the 0th station after the last,
if a passenger who got on at a station and wanted to get off at that same 
station did a full cycle on train before getting off, if the program worked the
same on both standard input and command file input, and if the program would 
stop even when "m f" was not the last command in the commands file. I tested 
all of these by created files with the standard output of both the demo and 
my code with the same commands and then diffed to see if there were any 
differences. I did this for all the testing files I created. Finally, I would
try to run invalid commmands on my program and the demo to make sure the error
messages were exactly the same between then two. A bug I ran into was my sim's
while loop wouldn't end when "m f" wasn't in the command file, even though
one of my while loop conditions were "not instream.eof()". 

Part that you found most difficult: I found the testing the part to be the 
most difficult, because it was difficult for me to isolate certain features of 
my program to test and to think of different edge cases and situation to test  
my code against. 

Time Spent: I spent 8 hours. 
